WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.901
-Our first guest is one of
our absolute favorites.

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:05.968
He's an Oscar-winning actor
and the author of the number-one

00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:08.400
"New York Times" best-selling
book, "Greenlights."

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.234
He has a brand-new children's
book, called "Just Because."

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:13.501
We're gonna
talk to him about that.

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:16.133
Please welcome,
Matthew McConaughey, everybody.

00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:17.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.501 --> 00:00:18.667
Yes!
Come on!

00:00:18.667 --> 00:00:21.834
-Jimmy, Jimmy, Jimmy,
don't call me James!

00:00:21.834 --> 00:00:23.434
-Matthew!
-What's happening?

00:00:23.434 --> 00:00:26.267
-You are on the show tonight
because I begged you to come on

00:00:26.267 --> 00:00:28.734
because I am so into this book.

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:33.234
And I know it's been out for --
is it a year?

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:34.634
-Almost two years now, I think.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:35.801
-Oh, my God.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:35.801 --> 00:00:38.834
I'm so sorry.
I'm so -- but I do love it.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:41.067
But I -- I'm so into the book.

00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:42.501
And I loved it,
and I read everything.

00:00:42.501 --> 00:00:47.801
It is -- It is charming,
it is funny, it is personal.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:50.801
You get in there.
You tell every single story.

00:00:50.801 --> 00:00:52.434
And I loved it.

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:54.501
And it's actually made me think.

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:56.067
It's just so fun.
-Awesome.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.767
-And then my friend said,
"Get the audiobook.

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.267
And you gotta hear it.
It's Matthew McConaughey

00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:01.734
talking and reading the book."

00:01:01.734 --> 00:01:03.901
I go, "This is gonna be
unbelievable!"

00:01:03.901 --> 00:01:05.067
[ Laughter ]

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:06.767
And it is unbelievable!

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.734
You are a fantastic storyteller.

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:10.701
Were you -- I have
so many questions for you,

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:13.133
but were you -- first of all,
were you reading the book

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.200
when you did the audiobook,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.534
or did you just remember it
from your head and go,

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:17.934
"I know these stories"?
-No, no, no.

00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:19.234
I was reading from the book.
-Okay, good.

00:01:19.234 --> 00:01:21.701
-I was reading from the book.
But I had worked so long

00:01:21.701 --> 00:01:24.334
on the book and the stories,
that --

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.133
They told me that when I
recorded the audio, they said,

00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:28.167
"You're gonna need
maybe 10 days."

00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:29.934
And I was like, "Oh,
that sounds like a long time.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:31.300
I mean,
I know all these stories."

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.667
And I hopped in the booth,
and I think I knocked it out

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:35.133
in 8 1/2 hours.

00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.434
So the total read's like 6:22,
but I knocked it out in a day.

00:01:39.434 --> 00:01:41.834
I kind of knew where the voices
were, where the characters were,

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:45.000
and I just laid back and said,
"Hey, if I'm sitting

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.267
next to a campfire with a beer
with you hanging out,

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.467
my story's will be here."

00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:50.734
-That's what it feels like,
yeah.

00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:54.100
And by the way, great Australian
accent in the book.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.901
-Thank you.
-I like hearing you do accents.

00:01:55.901 --> 00:01:57.801
It's good.
There's so many stories.

00:01:57.801 --> 00:02:00.067
I want you to just talk here
for eight hours,

00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:02.801
but I'm gonna try to get it down
to my favorite parts.

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:05.501
One that really stuck in my head
is when you're in high school

00:02:05.501 --> 00:02:07.934
and you had your truck.
-Yeah.

00:02:07.934 --> 00:02:11.434
-And -- And having that truck,
and what -- what did you --

00:02:11.434 --> 00:02:14.701
What was the truck?
What did you drive?

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:18.334
-The truck was a Dodge.
It was only, like,

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:21.767
a 4-cylinder Dodge truck
that was slightly lifted.

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:23.267
Good enough to go off-road,
though.

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:27.501
And I lived in East Texas.
So, after school, you know,

00:02:27.501 --> 00:02:29.834
girls and friends, we'd hop in
my truck and go muddin'.

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:31.200
You know what I mean?
That was --

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.000
You threw everything in the back
of the truck.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.601
You didn't care if you spilled
something in it.

00:02:34.601 --> 00:02:36.901
I was a truck guy.
It got muddy, you roll.

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:39.167
-And you had a CB radio
in it, too, right?

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:41.133
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.
Had a CB -- had a speaker

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.234
in the --
that I mounted in the grille.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:46.300
So I could pull up into the
parking lot in the morning

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.534
before school, and, like,
a friend of mine,

00:02:49.534 --> 00:02:52.400
this girl Cathy Cook
or something, I could get get

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.801
on my CB and like, go "Whoa!
Look at Cathy Cook this morning.

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:58.234
She is looking..."
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:58.234 --> 00:02:59.767
That'd go across
the whole parking lot.

00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:01.167
Everybody,
and she'd get embarrassed

00:03:01.167 --> 00:03:02.334
wondering where it come from,

00:03:02.334 --> 00:03:04.501
and then I'd hop up
and get busted.

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:06.667
So it was --
it was a lot of fun.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:10.234
The truck was a lot of fun.
-Yeah, you were a rascal.

00:03:10.234 --> 00:03:12.067
-I -- yeah, a fun rascal.
-Yeah.

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:13.767
-Fun rascal.
Here's what I did.

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:16.534
Here's where I snafu'd myself.

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:20.467
There was a beautiful cherry-red
300ZX, T-tops,

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:23.734
that went on sale across town,
and I drove by it and went in.

00:03:23.734 --> 00:03:26.634
And it was -- the guy said
he would trade me the truck in

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:29.467
for the 300ZX.
And I was like, "Ooh.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:32.667
Candy red, T-tops, 300ZX.
How 'bout that?"

00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:34.400
And I said yes.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.868
Big mistake.

00:03:35.868 --> 00:03:38.334
Instead of being the guy in
the front parking lot, talking,

00:03:38.334 --> 00:03:41.267
having fun, being the rascal,
and going mudding after school,

00:03:41.267 --> 00:03:44.934
I started parking my 300ZX off
in the back third parking lot,

00:03:44.934 --> 00:03:46.968
away from all the cars
'cause I didn't want anyone

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:49.234
to open the door and dent it,
right?

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:53.200
And I started, like,
leaning against my red 300ZX.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.934
Like,
"Isn't my red 300ZX so cool?

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:57.634
So therefore I'm so cool?"

00:03:57.634 --> 00:04:00.934
Well, I was no fun anymore.
I went dry, man.

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:02.234
The girls
didn't want to date me,

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:03.868
my buddies quit wanting
to hang out with me, like,

00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:07.033
"Dude. You're, like --
you're living off this 300ZX.

00:04:07.033 --> 00:04:10.567
You're no fun anymore, dude."
And I went back to that dealer,

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.501
pulled up and said, "This was
a bad life decision for me.

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:14.934
Can I please have my truck back?

00:04:14.934 --> 00:04:17.400
I'll give you the 300ZX back,
and some money if you want."

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.133
He said, "Okay."
I got the truck back.

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:20.667
I was back on the front lawn,

00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:22.501
messing with Cathy Cook
and going mudding after school.

00:04:22.501 --> 00:04:24.467
-Yeah, exactly.
"Greenlights."

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:26.100
[ Cheers and applause ]
"Greenlights."

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:32.100
-Yeah.
-I -- I like that you wrote down

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.901
bumper stickers that you think
are clever and funny

00:04:34.901 --> 00:04:36.434
throughout your life
in a journal.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:37.734
And then you just --
now and then you go, like,

00:04:37.734 --> 00:04:39.501
"Hey, here's a cool
bumper sticker I saw."

00:04:39.501 --> 00:04:40.801
And you just talk about that.

00:04:40.801 --> 00:04:44.400
I like the prescriptions --
prescribe!

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:45.868
-Yeah.
Prescribe!

00:04:45.868 --> 00:04:47.801
-Pre --oh, my gosh!

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:49.434
Hearing you say that,
I'm telling you,

00:04:49.434 --> 00:04:51.801
I spent 6 1/2 hours
with you recently,

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:53.734
and it's amazing
actually talking to you.

00:04:53.734 --> 00:04:57.701
It's almost bizarre.
It's almost like the metaverse.

00:04:57.701 --> 00:05:03.067
I like that you talk about your
mom and your dad and Rooster,

00:05:03.067 --> 00:05:04.868
who I love
and has been on the show.

00:05:04.868 --> 00:05:07.601
I didn't know how his name came
from -- 'cause he would just --

00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:09.400
no matter how long
everyone partied,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.300
he was the first one up.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.701
-Always up at sunrise.

00:05:12.701 --> 00:05:14.434
No matter if he partied
till 5:00 a.m.

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:17.734
Sun rose, Rooster's up.
-Yeah.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:21.567
Talking about your dad and
the way that they parented you,

00:05:21.567 --> 00:05:25.067
I know one of the things
is they want you to be honest.

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:28.133
And one of the things is
they don't want you to get --

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:29.868
they don't want you
to get caught.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:31.601
So, there's a couple things.
-Yeah.

00:05:31.601 --> 00:05:34.200
-You stole a pizza once,
and then your dad's like,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.901
"I'm not even mad
that you stole the pizza.

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:39.033
But then you lied to me
about it, and you got caught.

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:41.834
Don't --
My boy doesn't get caught."

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:44.234
-Like, "Son, if you're gonna try
to get away with that

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:46.100
and be that rascal
and get away with that mischief.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:47.801
I don't give a damn
about the stolen pizza,

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.701
but you've got to get better
at getting away with it, man."

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:50.834
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.834 --> 00:05:52.501
-And that's
when I got in trouble.

00:05:52.501 --> 00:05:55.100
And then there's parts of it
where you're laughing,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:56.601
and then there's parts
where it's just beautiful.

00:05:56.601 --> 00:06:00.234
But I think the treehouse one
stuck in my brain, too,

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:03.300
because you always
just wanted a treehouse.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.234
I mean, I think every kid
wants a treehouse,

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.767
if you have a backyard, if
you're lucky enough to have one.

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.767
-That was my dream.
You know, and as you know

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:11.467
in that story,
when you're a kid,

00:06:11.467 --> 00:06:14.367
I think a treehouse is a --
is a sort of great metaphor

00:06:14.367 --> 00:06:16.868
for all of our dreams
when you're a kid.

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:20.133
It's a place to explore.
You know, it's a place to

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:23.767
go have some privacy,
some adventure.

00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:25.801
It's about freedom,
it's about youth.

00:06:25.801 --> 00:06:28.167
And for me, that was my dream.
And I happened to come across

00:06:28.167 --> 00:06:30.434
a lumberyard,
where I got some lumber

00:06:30.434 --> 00:06:32.968
and built a 13-story treehouse
that was --

00:06:32.968 --> 00:06:35.534
I don't know how damn tall, but
it looked 100 feet tall to me

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:37.601
when I was done.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.601 --> 00:06:41.701
-Yeah. And you would go up in
the treehouse and just look out.

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.434
-Well, I'd go -- I remember I'd
make my lunch in the morning,

00:06:44.434 --> 00:06:46.300
and I'd take it
to the base of the tree.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:47.834
And I had this pulley
with a bucket at the bottom.

00:06:47.834 --> 00:06:49.100
And I put my lunch in.

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.133
I could have easily carried
my lunch up with me.

00:06:51.133 --> 00:06:53.400
But then I put it in the bucket,
and I'd climb all the way

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.100
to the 13th story and then just
have such a good time seeing --

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:57.901
"Look at this.
I can use a pulley to bring

00:06:57.901 --> 00:06:59.334
my lunch all the way up."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:01.868
-A good invention.
-And I'd sit there by myself

00:07:01.868 --> 00:07:03.934
and overlook --
it's the only place I could see

00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:06.367
all of Longview, where I lived,

00:07:06.367 --> 00:07:08.167
and it's the place where
I could see the horizon.

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:10.334
And it's actually
where I learned to even have

00:07:10.334 --> 00:07:11.934
more dreams
than just a treehouse.

00:07:11.934 --> 00:07:13.434
That was --
That was in the top --

00:07:13.434 --> 00:07:14.634
on the top floor
of that treehouse

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.400
is where I learned to dream.
-I love that.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:18.100
-Aww.
-I know. Isn't that amazing?

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.567
I just --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:21.567 --> 00:07:23.767
Let's talk about this children's
book and then look at the --

00:07:23.767 --> 00:07:25.067
Look at cover.

00:07:25.067 --> 00:07:26.467
It's the treehouse!
-There's a treehouse.

00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:28.501
-It's a treehouse!
[ Applause ]

00:07:28.501 --> 00:07:30.400
Stop it!
Just stop it!

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.968
It's a treehouse!
-It showed up again!

00:07:33.968 --> 00:07:37.601
It's not 13 stories, but, hey,
I think, again,

00:07:37.601 --> 00:07:41.267
that iconic image speaks to
the kid in all of us.

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.734
You know, as I said, for me,
it was a symbol of my youth,

00:07:43.734 --> 00:07:45.834
freedom, exploration, privacy.

00:07:45.834 --> 00:07:47.400
And it was my first big dream,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.167
and where I learned
to dream bigger,

00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:52.767
at the top of my own treehouse.
So, yeah, it made it to the --

00:07:52.767 --> 00:07:55.267
to the cover of "Just Because,"
my new children's book

00:07:55.267 --> 00:07:57.934
that I got coming out.
-Look at you.

00:07:57.934 --> 00:08:00.200
You're crushing it now.
And I love it, 'cause it's

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:02.667
kind of a -- it's --
it's very "Greenlights,"

00:08:02.667 --> 00:08:04.968
but it's for kids.
It's giving advice.

00:08:04.968 --> 00:08:06.200
-Yeah.
-You know what it

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:08.133
reminded me of?
And -- I don't want to say --

00:08:08.133 --> 00:08:10.567
Obviously, it's the best,
but "The Giving Tree" almost,

00:08:10.567 --> 00:08:12.200
where it's like --
-Uh-huh.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.501
-You know, you're reading these
lines, and you're like, "Oh."

00:08:14.501 --> 00:08:17.400
And it just hits you.
And it's -- it's funny.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.934
It's great, it's smart,
it's emotional, it's everything.

00:08:20.934 --> 00:08:23.734
And it's almost like a --
like a this-or-that book,

00:08:23.734 --> 00:08:26.501
which is what I like with kids
books 'cause it's simple.

00:08:26.501 --> 00:08:28.334
Everyone can read it.
Fathers can read it,

00:08:28.334 --> 00:08:29.667
mothers can read it --
everyone can read it.

00:08:29.667 --> 00:08:31.901
And you get it and you go, like,

00:08:31.901 --> 00:08:35.701
"Just because I'm finished
doesn't mean that I'm done."

00:08:35.701 --> 00:08:38.133
Like, I love that.
That is a great one.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:39.634
[ Applause ]

00:08:39.634 --> 00:08:43.167
-Just because you got the goal
doesn't mean that you have won.

00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:45.367
-Yeah.
-Just because you wrote it

00:08:45.367 --> 00:08:47.601
doesn't mean that I read it.

00:08:47.601 --> 00:08:50.734
Just because I did it again
doesn't mean I don't regret it.

00:08:50.734 --> 00:08:51.901
[ Laughter ]

00:08:51.901 --> 00:08:53.601
-Come on, bud.
Come on.

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:56.234
That's what I'm talking about.
-Things like that, you know?

00:08:56.234 --> 00:08:58.100
-I could talk to you
for eight hours.

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:00.300
I love that you came back
and did this for me.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.367
I appreciate this.
I also want to talk

00:09:02.367 --> 00:09:03.601
to you quickly --
I don't even know --

00:09:03.601 --> 00:09:05.601
Can we talk about other projects
that you're doing?

00:09:05.601 --> 00:09:08.934
But there's one out there
that everyone's talking about.

00:09:08.934 --> 00:09:10.934
Are you playing Elvis?

00:09:10.934 --> 00:09:13.968
-Yeah, "Agent Elvis."
-Agent Elvis?

00:09:13.968 --> 00:09:15.501
-Agent Elvis.
-Yes.

00:09:15.501 --> 00:09:20.834
-Yeah, I get to voice the global
rock 'n' roll icon, Elvis,

00:09:20.834 --> 00:09:23.033
by day, who, in "Agent Elvis,"

00:09:23.033 --> 00:09:24.801
is an ass-kicking vigilante
by night.

00:09:24.801 --> 00:09:26.200
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.801
-And I've got a chimpanzee
as my crime-fighting sidekick.

00:09:29.801 --> 00:09:31.667
-Yeah!
That's what I'm talking about.

00:09:31.667 --> 00:09:34.133
Why would you not do that?
Only you could --

00:09:34.133 --> 00:09:36.167
Come on,
only you could pull that off.

00:09:36.167 --> 00:09:37.534
It's always great to see you,
bud.

00:09:37.534 --> 00:09:39.067
-It's out in March on Netflix.

00:09:39.067 --> 00:09:41.801
-I appreciate you coming on,
and best to the family.

00:09:41.801 --> 00:09:45.167
And thank you, thank you so much
for this book and for this book.

00:09:45.167 --> 00:09:47.133
Really kind of changed my life,
pal.

